WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.211
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.511 --> 00:00:08.828
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.128 --> 00:00:15.449
Unit seven: Corporate Governance. Today you will define governance roles with defining relative clauses (who / that).

4
00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:18.262
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.526
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.383 --> 00:00:24.721
Board of directors.

7
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:29.632
Board of directors means the group elected to oversee a company's management.

8
00:00:30.217 --> 00:00:33.865
The board of directors replaced the CEO after the scandal.

9
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.253
Your turn. Say: Board of directors.

10
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.519
Board of directors.

11
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:43.308
Shareholder.

12
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:48.627
Shareholder means a person or institution that owns shares in a company.

13
00:00:49.217 --> 00:00:51.599
Shareholders voted against the pay increase.

14
00:00:52.183 --> 00:00:55.335
Your turn. Say: Shareholder.

15
00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.271
Shareholder.

16
00:01:00.017 --> 00:01:01.366
Fiduciary duty.

17
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:07.473
Fiduciary duty means a legal obligation to act in another party's best interest.

18
00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:11.650
Directors have a fiduciary duty to the company, not to themselves.

19
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.949
Your turn. Say: Fiduciary duty.

20
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.091
Fiduciary duty.

21
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.025
Transparency.

22
00:01:22.017 --> 00:01:25.502
Transparency means openness about decisions and information.

23
00:01:26.117 --> 00:01:29.068
Transparency about fees rebuilt customer trust.

24
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:32.475
Your turn. Say: Transparency.

25
00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:36.706
Transparency.

26
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.579
Compliance.

27
00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:43.696
Compliance means following laws, regulations and internal rules.

28
00:01:44.283 --> 00:01:47.490
The compliance team checks every new product.

29
00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:50.678
Your turn. Say: Compliance.

30
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:54.662
Compliance.

31
00:01:55.417 --> 00:01:56.499
Oversight.

32
00:01:57.483 --> 00:02:00.655
Oversight means supervision to ensure proper conduct.

33
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.225
Weak oversight allowed the fraud to continue for years.

34
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:07.144
Your turn. Say: Oversight.

35
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:10.920
Oversight.

36
00:02:11.683 --> 00:02:12.963
Accountability.

37
00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.772
Accountability means being answerable for one's decisions and actions.

38
00:02:18.383 --> 00:02:22.647
Accountability means accepting consequences, not just praise.

39
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.750
Your turn. Say: Accountability.

40
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:30.694
Accountability.

41
00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:32.474
Ethics.

42
00:02:33.483 --> 00:02:36.528
Ethics means moral principles that guide behaviour.

43
00:02:37.117 --> 00:02:40.742
A code of ethics guides employees facing difficult choices.

44
00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.933
Your turn. Say: Ethics.

45
00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:47.837
Ethics.

46
00:02:48.633 --> 00:02:52.574
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:53.417 --> 00:02:58.423
Visal, why did the board remove the finance director even though profits were rising?

48
00:02:59.517 --> 00:03:07.370
Because he approved loans to a company that his brother owned. A director who has a conflict of interest must declare it.

49
00:03:08.483 --> 00:03:11.078
But the loans were repaid. Where was the harm?

50
00:03:12.183 --> 00:03:21.674
The harm is the breach of fiduciary duty. Directors are trusted to act for shareholders, not for people that they personally favour.

51
00:03:22.783 --> 00:03:25.096
How do boards catch problems like that?

52
00:03:26.183 --> 00:03:35.451
Through independent members who have no ties to management, and an audit committee that reviews every related-party transaction.

53
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:37.996
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:42.626
Grammar focus: Defining relative clauses (who and that).

55
00:03:42.926 --> 00:03:44.206
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:44.917 --> 00:03:48.391
A board that upholds fiduciary duty protects shareholders.

57
00:03:49.283 --> 00:03:52.200
A director who has a conflict of interest must declare it.

58
00:03:53.117 --> 00:03:56.591
Companies that publish clear reports attract more investors.

59
00:03:57.533 --> 00:04:08.150
Defining relative clauses identify which person or thing we mean, so no commas are used. Use 'who' for people and 'that' or 'which' for things; 'that' is also possible for people informally.

60
00:04:08.917 --> 00:04:12.904
Be careful! Don't say: A director which lies must resign.

61
00:04:14.867 --> 00:04:17.505
Say: A director who lies must resign.

62
00:04:17.805 --> 00:04:19.038
Use 'who' for people.

63
00:04:19.833 --> 00:04:22.544
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:23.333 --> 00:04:25.043
Who Watches the Managers?.

65
00:04:25.343 --> 00:04:31.104
Corporate governance is the system of rules and practices that determines how a company is directed and controlled.

66
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:38.221
Shareholders who own a company rarely run it themselves, so they elect a board that supervises management on their behalf.

67
00:04:39.233 --> 00:04:44.101
Directors owe a fiduciary duty, meaning they must place the company's interests above their own.

68
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:51.418
Scandals in which executives hid losses have shown that weak oversight can destroy firms that once seemed unshakeable.

69
00:04:52.433 --> 00:05:00.249
As Cambodia's stock exchange grows, companies that embrace transparency and independent boards are likely to win investors' trust.

70
00:05:01.333 --> 00:05:05.033
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:05.883 --> 00:05:08.754
A manager blank hides losses breaks the rules.

72
00:05:09.054 --> 00:05:12.370
A, which. B, who. C, whose.

73
00:05:15.933 --> 00:05:17.515
The answer is B: who.

74
00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:23.018
A legal obligation to act in another party's best interest is a blank.

75
00:05:23.318 --> 00:05:27.680
A, fiduciary duty. B, compliance. C, shareholder.

76
00:05:31.233 --> 00:05:33.558
The answer is A: fiduciary duty.

77
00:05:34.717 --> 00:05:37.633
Supervision to ensure proper conduct is blank.

78
00:05:37.933 --> 00:05:42.272
A, ethics. B, transparency. C, oversight.

79
00:05:45.833 --> 00:05:47.903
The answer is C: oversight.

80
00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.736
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:51.036 --> 00:05:57.824
A, Firms, that cheat, lose trust.. B, Firms that cheat lose trust.. C, Firms who cheats lose trust..

82
00:06:01.367 --> 00:06:04.736
The answer is B: Firms that cheat lose trust..

83
00:06:05.883 --> 00:06:09.381
According to the reading, shareholders elect a board because they…

84
00:06:09.681 --> 00:06:15.981
A, rarely run the company themselves. B, want to avoid taxes. C, manage daily operations.

85
00:06:19.533 --> 00:06:22.880
The answer is A: rarely run the company themselves.

86
00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:27.485
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:32.370
What is a 'fiduciary duty' and why does it matter for corporate leaders?

88
00:06:49.183 --> 00:06:50.544
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:50.844 --> 00:07:25.548
A fiduciary duty is a legal and moral obligation to act in the best interests of the people who have trusted you with their money or property. For corporate leaders, it means placing the company and its shareholders above personal gain. It matters because directors who misuse their position can destroy value that thousands of investors depend on. A director who secretly awards contracts to a relative betrays that trust, even if they are profitable. Without this duty, investment would rest on blind faith.

90
00:07:25.848 --> 00:07:27.453
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:33.789
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and defining relative clauses (who and that).

92
00:07:34.089 --> 00:07:39.318
Today's value is accountability. Take responsibility for your decisions and their results.

93
00:07:39.618 --> 00:07:42.097
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:42.397 --> 00:07:46.071
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

